# Async module lessons

Use when drafting or regenerating **Canvas async module pages** under `courses/*/modules/`.

## Student-facing voice

- Student-facing only: no teacher construction meta, no “why we built it this way,” no scaffolding-of-the-course voice.
- Do **not** show section weightings (e.g. `~20% of module`) on the student HTML.
- Core motivation: **math can be valuable for understanding life**.
- Write like Sal Khan: thorough but concise; patient and clear; explain *why*; one idea at a time; concrete then generalize. Avoid dense textbook speak and fluff.
- Online ELC: the async page must **stand alone** if a live class is missed.
- Align to the course syllabus story/artifact ethos and that course’s `course-plan.md` target IA.

## Instructional sequencing

- No ahead-of-schedule vocabulary or notation.
- Example: do not use function notation \(f(x)\) (or lean on “function” as assumed knowledge) before the sequence introduces the idea, then the notation.
- Audit stems, solutions, intros, hooks, and Explore notes for premature terms (vertex form, zeros jargon, etc.) relative to where the student is in the module.

## Delivery shape

- One **Canvas-like** single HTML preview page per module for testing (readable content width; not an app shell).
- Prefer local `python3 -m http.server` for preview so embeds work (`file://` often blocks iframes).
- HTML is **regenerated from a sqlite question DB** via a build script. Do not treat generated HTML as source of truth.

## In-repo media assets (AU)

- Save module images (thumbnails, diagrams, Explore stills you create) **in the repo** beside the async HTML, e.g. `courses/<CODE>/modules/<module>/async/assets/` (or `media/`).
- Reference them with **relative paths** (`assets/foo.svg`) so Canvas porting can ship files with the page — do **not** rely only on remote URLs for assets you own.
- Prefer SVG/PNG committed under that folder; keep filenames stable.

## Question bank

- Store all examples / formatives / practice in sqlite (seed → DB → render).
- Render each item as a **question card** with curriculum expectation footnotes (codes + short titles from the course curriculum DB).
- Every item has a stable **smart ID** seeded explicitly (e.g. `M1-S2-E01` = Module · Section · E/F/P · number). IDs must survive regenerations.
- Also generate a front-facing **question bank browse page** (search/filter by ID, section, type, expectation) with links/anchors back to cards on the lesson page.

## Practice accordion

- Bundle **similar-topic practice** cards in collapsed `<details class="practice-accordion">` groups (seed `cluster_title`; summary like `Factoring (5)`).
- Keeps the page compact while preserving natural progression when the student opens each accordion.
- Examples stay expanded (not accordioned) so the core teach path stays visible.

## Solution toggles (AU)

- On example and practice solution `<details>` toggles, use the label **“Show solution”** (not “Show worked solution,” “Reveal full solution,” or similar).

## Formative accordion (AU)

- Bundle formative checks in collapsed `<details class="formative-accordion">` groups — **all groups start collapsed, including the first**.
- Do **not** set `open` on the first formative group. Summary should name the check (title + count when clustered).

## Explore media

- Prefer **Khan Academy** embeds when they match the subtopic; verify URLs exist.
- Fallback: **Steve Sweeney** YouTube (`@stevesweeney70`) — verify video IDs; do not invent.
- **Desmos** for transformations-style exploration (and table/graph check work).
- Multiple media: use **tabs only when they cover the same content** (same idea, alternate modality). If media are different ideas or sequential beats, render **separate sequential Explore blocks** — never tab unrelated resources together.
- Store same-content clustering in the DB (e.g. `content_group` + `block_title`).

## Learning-modality instructions (AU — Khan / Sweeney)

- Resource cards use a **one-line standard instruction**, not verbose chrome that restates title + long notes.
- **Khan Academy:** instruction line like “Watch and complete activities here” + keep **Open on Khan Academy** (or equivalent open-resource CTA).
- **Steve Sweeney / YouTube:** instruction line like “Watch the following video” + keep **Open resource** link.
- Embed iframe (when present) follows below. Author `notes` in the DB may stay for seed context; do not dump them into the student chrome line.

## Desmos activity ask + how-to (AU — all modules)

- Never drop a bare Desmos iframe/link.
- Show an **overall activity instruction** up front (what to do / what to notice — from seed `notes` or dedicated activity-ask field).
- Put calculator **how-to / interaction steps** in an expandable `<details>` (patient click/drag/type/slider/watch/reset steps). Seed `interaction_steps_html` on each `kind=desmos` resource; renderer must require steps and place them inside that expandable — not as a always-open wall of text.
- Tailor steps to that specific activity (blank calculator vs saved graph with sliders, table work vs transformations, etc.).
- Question cards that ask students to use Desmos (examples/practice) should also spell out interaction steps in the stem, not only “open Desmos.”

## Title / type hierarchy (AU)

- Use a clear typographic scale in module CSS — not flat same-size headers:
  - **Page title** (`h1` / hero): largest
  - **Section** (`h2`): next
  - **Explore / block** (`h3`): clearly smaller than section
  - **Card titles** (`h4` / accordion summaries): smaller still, purposeful weight
- Prefer the established type scale in `async/styles.css`; do not invent one-off sizes per section.

## Expectation footnotes

- Keep footnotes **quiet**: small muted codes (hover/`title` may carry the full statement).
- Stay **Canvas-portable**: put codes on `data-expectations` (on the card and/or footnote) plus a subtle visible fallback so import does not drop the data.
- Do **not** use loud “Curriculum expectations” list blocks on student cards.

## Difference tables

- Whenever first differences (and second differences, if shown) are computed or displayed, use a proper HTML `<table>` with clear headers (e.g. \(x\), \(y\), 1st diff, 2nd diff). Never ASCII/monospace “tables.”
- Style tables for Canvas-like readability in the module CSS.
